url: /orm/extensions/paradedb
metaTitle: ParadeDB full-text search | Prisma ORM
metaDescription: 'Add the ParadeDB extension to a Prisma ORM 8 project: declare a BM25 index in a TypeScript contract, then search with paradeDbMatch, paradeDbPhrase, and paradeDbTerm and rank results with paradeDbScore.'
---

The ParadeDB extension adds full-text search to Prisma ORM 8, with results ranked by how well they match. It uses `pg_search`, a PostgreSQL extension from [ParadeDB](https://paradedb.com). `pg_search` adds a BM25 index, which ranks results the way most search engines do. You declare one index over the text columns to search, and then filter and rank rows by a search query.

`@prisma/orm-extension-paradedb` is experimental: what it offers may change in a later Prisma ORM release, so check its release notes before you upgrade.

This page assumes a Prisma ORM 8 project on PostgreSQL, such as one from [Create a new app with PostgreSQL](/prisma-orm/quickstart/postgresql), where `src/prisma/db.ts` creates the client your code queries with.

`pg_search` has to be installed on your PostgreSQL server. Check that your provider offers it; [ParadeDB's documentation](https://www.paradedb.com/docs) lists where it runs. Prisma Postgres includes `pg_search` 0.17, which supports `paradeDbMatch` but not the other search operations or `paradeDbScore`, and needs one change to its search path; see [Full-text search with ParadeDB](/postgres/database/paradedb#use-paradedb-with-prisma-orm-8) for the workarounds. To run it on your machine, use the `paradedb/paradedb` Docker image: [A database on your machine](/prisma-orm/local-database#postgresql-in-docker) shows the Compose file, and you change its `image:` to `paradedb/paradedb`.

## Your contract must be TypeScript

The search index can only be declared in a TypeScript contract, `src/prisma/contract.ts`; a `contract.prisma` contract cannot declare it yet. A project has one contract, so if yours is `contract.prisma`, rewrite it in TypeScript and delete `contract.prisma`: [TypeScript schema builder](/orm/contract-authoring/typescript-schema-builder) shows the TypeScript form of everything you can write in `contract.prisma`. Then change `contract` in `prisma.config.ts` to `./src/prisma/contract.ts`. When the rewrite describes the same tables, `migration plan` finds nothing to change.

## Set it up

Install the package next to `@prisma/orm-postgres`:

```npm
npm install @prisma/orm-extension-paradedb
```

In a project that `npm create prisma` generated, that command fails with `ERESOLVE`, because the project's Prisma Composer packages require the exact version of `@prisma/orm-postgres` that it installed, and the extension's latest version requires a newer one. Install the extension at the version of `@prisma/orm-postgres` in your `package.json` instead:

```bash
npm install --save-exact "@prisma/orm-extension-paradedb@$(npm pkg get 'dependencies.@prisma/orm-postgres' | tr -d '"')"
```

A Prisma ORM 8 extension is a package that both the `prisma` command-line tool and your app's client load, not a Prisma ORM 7 client extension made with `$extends`. Add it in three places: in `prisma.config.ts` from `/control`, in `src/prisma/db.ts` from `/runtime`, and in the contract from `/pack`, the part `defineContract` uses. If `npm create prisma` generated your project, do not replace these files. In `prisma.config.ts`, add the `/control` import and `extensions: [paradedb]` inside the `ormConfig({ ... })` call. The generated file also has an `extensions` array inside `composer({ ... })`, which is for Prisma Composer and is the wrong place. In `src/prisma/db.ts`, add the `/runtime` import and `extensions: [paradedb]` to every `postgres(...)` call. `npm run dev:composer` cannot run this extension: the local database it starts is PGlite, which does not include `pg_search`, so the extension's migration fails with `MIGRATION.EXECUTION_FAILED`. Start the app with `npm run dev` instead, with `DATABASE_URL` set to a PostgreSQL server that has `pg_search`, such as the `paradedb/paradedb` Docker image above. The smallest files that work look like this:

```ts title="prisma.config.ts"
import 'dotenv/config';
import { definePrismaConfig } from 'prisma/config';
import paradedb from '@prisma/orm-extension-paradedb/control';
import { defineConfig as ormConfig } from '@prisma/orm-postgres/config';

export default definePrismaConfig({
  orm: ormConfig({
    contract: './src/prisma/contract.ts',
    extensions: [paradedb],
    db: { connection: process.env['DATABASE_URL']! },
  }),
});
```

```ts title="src/prisma/db.ts"
import 'dotenv/config';
import paradedb from '@prisma/orm-extension-paradedb/runtime';
import postgres from '@prisma/orm-postgres/runtime';
import { contractFromJson } from '@prisma/orm-postgres/static';
import type { Contract } from './contract.d';
import contractJson from './contract.json' with { type: 'json' };

export const db = postgres({
  contract: contractFromJson<Contract>(contractJson),
  url: process.env['DATABASE_URL']!,
  extensions: [paradedb],
});
```

## Declare the search index

In the contract, pass the pack to `defineContract` with the key `paradedb`, and add the index with `constraints.index`:

```ts title="src/prisma/contract.ts"
import { defineContract } from '@prisma/orm-postgres/contract-builder';
import paradedb from '@prisma/orm-extension-paradedb/pack';

export const contract = defineContract({ extensions: { paradedb } }, ({ field, model, type }) => {
  const Article = model('Article', {
    fields: {
      id: field.column(type.Int()).id(),
      title: field.column(type.String()),
      body: field.column(type.String()),
    },
  });

  return {
    models: {
      Article: Article.sql(({ cols, constraints }) => ({
        indexes: [
          constraints.index([cols.id, cols.title, cols.body], {
            type: 'bm25',
            options: { key_field: 'id' },
            name: 'article_search',
          }),
        ],
      })),
    },
  };
});
```

List the key column and every column you want to search. `key_field` names the column that identifies each row, usually the primary key. The package requires it, although `pg_search` 0.26 and newer ignore it. The table is named after the model, `Article`, as it would be from `contract.prisma`.

Then update the generated files, write the migration, and apply it to your development database:

```bash
npx prisma contract emit
npx prisma migration plan --name add-search
npx prisma db migrate --advance-ref db
```

In a project that `npm create prisma` generated, two things differ:

- The project has a first migration but no `db` ref, so `migration plan` stops with `MIGRATION.PLAN_ORIGIN_UNKNOWN`. Unless you have run `npm run db:init` in this checkout, run `npx prisma migration ref set db <directory>` once before `migration plan`, with the name of the first directory that `ls migrations/app` lists, such as `20261120T1155_init`.
- `db migrate` applies the migrations to the database in `DATABASE_URL`, so set it to the server with `pg_search` first.

`migration plan` writes your migration under `migrations/app/`, and `contract emit` has already written the extension's files under `migrations/paradedb/`, whose migration installs `pg_search` in the database. Commit both. `pg_search` may already be installed in another schema on the connection's search path, and `db sign` and `db verify` accept it there. Do not declare `extension pg_search {}` in your own contract, because the extension declares it. [Generating a migration](/orm/migrations/generating-a-migration) explains `--advance-ref db`.

## Search

Each text column in the index has search operations, which you use in `where()`. A search splits the column's text and your query into words, ignoring case:

| Operation | Matches rows whose field |
| --- | --- |
| `body.paradeDbMatch(query)` or `body.paradeDbMatchAny(query)` | contains any word of `query` |
| `body.paradeDbMatchAll(query)` | contains every word of `query` |
| `body.paradeDbPhrase(query)` | contains the words of `query` next to each other, in order |
| `body.paradeDbTerm(word)` | contains the one word `word`, compared as written, so give it in lowercase |

```ts
const articles = await db.orm.public.Article.where((a) => a.body.paradeDbMatch('migration graph')).all();
```

To search the title and the body with one query, combine two matches with `or`:

```ts
import { or } from '@prisma/orm-postgres/orm-client';

const query = 'migration graph';
const articles = await db.orm.public.Article.where((a) =>
  or(a.title.paradeDbMatch(query), a.body.paradeDbMatch(query)),
).all();
```

To rank results, write the query with the [SQL query builder](/orm/fundamentals/advanced-queries). There, `f` holds the table's columns, and `fns` holds the same operations, which take the column as their first argument. `paradeDbScore` takes the key column and returns each row's relevance, higher for a better match. It needs a ParadeDB filter in the same query, as this one has; without one, PostgreSQL refuses the query with `pdb.score must be evaluated by a ParadeDB scan`:

```ts
const plan = db.sql.public.Article.select('id', 'title')
  .select('score', (f, fns) => fns.paradeDbScore(f.id))
  .where((f, fns) => fns.paradeDbMatch(f.body, 'migration graph'))
  .orderBy((f, fns) => fns.paradeDbScore(f.id), { direction: 'desc' })
  .build();

const ranked = await db.query(plan).toArray();
// [{ id: 3, title: 'Migrations explained', score: 1.478 }, { id: 1, title: 'Prisma ORM 8 is here', score: 0.43 }]
```

In the SQL query builder, `fns` also has operations that change how a query matches. Each one takes your query text and returns a new query, which you pass to a search operation in place of the text:

| Operation | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `fns.paradeDbFuzzy(word, distance)` | Also matches words that are up to `distance` single-character changes (an added, removed, or replaced character) away from `word`. `distance` is 0, 1, or 2. |
| `fns.paradeDbSlop(query, slop)` | Pass it to `paradeDbPhrase`. Also matches the phrase with up to `slop` other words between its words. `slop` is 0 or more. |
| `fns.paradeDbProximity(word).within(distance, other)` | Pass it to `paradeDbMatch`. Matches `word` and `other` with at most `distance` other words between them, in either order. `distance` is 0 or more. Pass `{ ordered: true }` as a third argument to `within` to require that order, and chain more `.within(...)` calls for more words. |
| `fns.paradeDbBoost(query, weight)` | Weights the `paradeDbScore` of the rows this query matches by `weight`, an integer from -2048 to 2048. Use it in one part of a `fns.or(...)`, such as the title match, so those rows rank higher, as the example below shows. |
| `fns.paradeDbConst(query, value)` | Gives every row this query matches the same `paradeDbScore`, `value`, an integer. |

{/* cspell:ignore migraton */}

```ts
const plan = db.sql.public.Article.select('id')
  .where((f, fns) => fns.paradeDbMatch(f.body, fns.paradeDbFuzzy('migraton', 1)))
  .orderBy((f) => f.id)
  .build();

const rows = await db.query(plan).toArray();
// [{ id: 1 }, { id: 3 }]: the two articles from the ranked example, which both contain "migration"
```

`paradeDbSlop` and `paradeDbProximity` go into a phrase search and a match:

```ts
const nearby = db.sql.public.Article.select('id')
  .where((f, fns) => fns.paradeDbMatch(f.body, fns.paradeDbProximity('migration').within(3, 'graph')))
  .build();

const loosePhrase = db.sql.public.Article.select('id')
  .where((f, fns) => fns.paradeDbPhrase(f.body, fns.paradeDbSlop('migration graph', 2)))
  .build();
```

To rank title matches above body matches, boost the title part of a `fns.or(...)`:

```ts
const boosted = db.sql.public.Article.select('id', 'title')
  .select('score', (f, fns) => fns.paradeDbScore(f.id))
  .where((f, fns) => fns.or(fns.paradeDbMatch(f.title, fns.paradeDbBoost('migrations', 2)), fns.paradeDbMatch(f.body, 'migration')))
  .orderBy((f, fns) => fns.paradeDbScore(f.id), { direction: 'desc' })
  .build();
```

A value outside the range in the table, or a number that is not an integer, throws an error whose `code` is `PARADEDB.ARGUMENT_INVALID`, before anything is sent to the database.

